# ApplyPilot environment configuration
#
# Copy this file to ~/.applypilot/.env and fill in your OWN keys:
#     mkdir -p ~/.applypilot && cp .env.example ~/.applypilot/.env
#
# ApplyPilot reads ~/.applypilot/.env — NOT this repo. (A .env in the repo root
# is also read as a fallback, but the one in ~/.applypilot wins. Don't keep both;
# it only creates confusion about which key is live.)
#
# Verify with:  applypilot doctor


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 1. LLM provider — REQUIRED. Pick exactly one block.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Used for scoring, tailoring, and cover letters. Detection order lives in
# llm.py:_detect_provider() — note that setting LLM_URL makes it skip Gemini and
# OpenAI entirely, even if their keys are set below.

# --- Option A: Gemini (recommended — generous free tier) ---
# Get a key at https://aistudio.google.com/apikey
GEMINI_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=gemini-3.1-flash-lite   # optional; this is the default

# --- Option B: OpenAI ---
# OPENAI_API_KEY=
# LLM_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini             # optional; this is the default

# --- Option C: any OpenAI-compatible endpoint (OpenRouter, Ollama, llama.cpp) ---
# Setting LLM_URL overrides options A and B.
# LLM_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# LLM_MODEL=z-ai/glm-5.3-flash
# LLM_API_KEY=sk-or-...
#
# Local example:
# LLM_URL=http://127.0.0.1:8080/v1
# LLM_MODEL=local-model


# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 2. OpenRouter — REQUIRED for `applypilot apply`
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The default apply backend is Goose, which drives the browser on a cheap
# OpenRouter model (~$0.05 per application) instead of your Claude
# subscription quota. Get a key at https://openrouter.ai/keys
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=

# The same key also serves as an automatic fallback for the scoring/tailoring
# stages: if you're on Gemini (option A) and its daily free quota runs out,
# ApplyPilot retries on OpenRouter's free auto-router rather than dying
# mid-run. That only fires after Gemini's own retries are exhausted.
#
# The apply model itself is `goose_model` in ~/.applypilot/settings.json
# (default: xiaomi/mimo-v2.5), not an environment variable.

# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# 3. Auto-apply extras — OPTIONAL
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# CAPTCHA solving during apply. Without it, jobs behind a CAPTCHA are marked
# 'captcha' and skipped rather than failing the run.
# CAPSOLVER_API_KEY=

# Override Chrome detection if it isn't in a standard location.
# CHROME_PATH=

# Proxy for job-board scraping, if you get rate-limited.
# PROXY=host:port:user:pass

# Residential proxy for the apply session (Chrome + CAPTCHA solving), separate
# from the scraping PROXY above. Leave unset to keep today's behavior (no
# proxy, CapSolver solves ProxyLess).
#
# Current setup: routes through the Pi (jakubpi, 100.125.177.85 on Tailscale)
# via scripts/pi_relay.py -- see scripts/pi-relay.service and
# scripts/vm-capsolver-relay.service for how both hops run persistently.
# Chrome's local forwarder (chrome.py) talks to APPLY_PROXY directly over
# Tailscale, no public exposure needed for that leg. CapSolver can't reach
# Tailscale addresses, so its "proxy" field is pointed at
# APPLY_PROXY_PUBLIC_HOST instead -- the one hop with a port actually open
# to the internet (see scripts/vm_capsolver_relay.py), which itself just
# forwards on to APPLY_PROXY over Tailscale. Same shared secret both hops.
# No "{session}" needed here -- the Pi has exactly one fixed IP, not a
# rotating pool, so there's nothing to pin per job; {session} substitution
# only matters if APPLY_PROXY ever points at a paid vendor pool instead.
#
# APPLY_PROXY=100.125.177.85:18888:applypilot:<PI_RELAY_PASSWORD below>
# APPLY_PROXY_PUBLIC_HOST=<oracle VM's public IP>
# APPLY_PROXY_PUBLIC_PORT=18889
#
# The Pi's relay password -- must match on both scripts/pi_relay.py (the Pi)
# and scripts/vm_capsolver_relay.py (the VM). Generate a real random secret,
# don't ship the example value.
# PI_RELAY_USER=applypilot
# PI_RELAY_PASSWORD=
#
# Proxy protocol CapSolver should use to connect upstream. "http" is correct
# for this setup (pi_relay.py speaks plain HTTP CONNECT).
# APPLY_PROXY_TYPE=http
